**Verdict — UNVERIFIED as a literature-completeness claim:** I found no verified subexponential classical algorithm satisfying your exclusion of order, smoothness, and relation methods. I also cannot prove that every arithmetic route through quadratic forms is an order problem.

**PROVED:** A useful narrower barrier is available. In a precisely defined generic group model, producing new 2-torsion necessarily produces a nonzero relation among the original inputs. In a cyclic quotient of order \(2P\), it produces an odd multiple of \(P\). A distributional lower bound of \(\Omega(P^{1/3})\) follows by a standard formal-word argument. A general \(\Omega(\sqrt P)\) claim would be too strong in the usual model.

**PROVED / KNOWN:** The main unresolved opportunity lies in the integer coefficients of forms and ideals. SQUFOF exploits those coefficients in a way that a black-box class group cannot express. Giant steps accelerate positioning and the return phase; they do not currently provide a subexponential method for locating a useful square form.

Labels below apply to the whole paragraph or table row. “PROVED” means proved here under the stated assumptions. “KNOWN, conditional on GRH” labels a conditional theorem, not GRH itself. No novelty is claimed for the elementary reductions or model arguments.

## 0. Conventions that affect the answer

**KNOWN:** For \(D<0\), use the proper invertible ideal class group of the order \(\mathcal O_D\). For \(D>0\), proper form classes correspond to a narrow class group; the ordinary class group and the principal infrastructure circle require separate treatment. An ambiguous **class**, an ambiguous **reduced form**, and a reflection point of the infrastructure are different objects. See Stevenhagen, *Rédei reciprocity, governing fields and negative Pell*, §§2–3, for the narrow-class-group conventions and genus maps. [Stevenhagen 2022](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/128E98F4B96AB15B77CDF63E6E27D447/S0305004121000335a.pdf/redei-reciprocity-governing-fields-and-negative-pell.pdf)

**PROVED:** Factor the small multiplier and first compute \(\gcd(k,N)\). Thereafter assume \(\gcd(k,N)=1\). Because \(N\) is promised squarefree, the fundamental discriminant and conductor associated with \(\pm4kN\) can be determined without splitting \(N\).

**PROVED:** Let
\[
G=\operatorname{Cl}(D),\qquad T=G[2],\qquad A\le T
\]
where \(A\) is the subgroup generated by the supplied public ambiguous classes. The abstract target is \(T\setminus A\), and requires the promise \(A\ne T\). Membership in \(A\) can be checked by enumeration: its size is bounded by a polynomial in \(k\), hence by \(\operatorname{poly}(\log N)\).

**PROVED:** For real forms, obtaining a useful ambiguous representative can reveal a factor even when its ordinary ideal class is principal. Consequently, a lower bound for outputting a new element of \(G[2]\) does not automatically cover the full infrastructure target in your question.

## 1. Access to the 2-primary part

### 1(a). Genus characters without the factors

**KNOWN:** For a fundamental discriminant
\[
D=d_1\cdots d_t
\]
written as a product of prime discriminants, genus characters have the form
\[
\chi_{d_i}([F])=\left(\frac{d_i}{r}\right),
\]
where \(r\) is primitively represented by \(F\) and coprime to \(D\). They describe \(G/G^2\), and the principal genus is \(G^2\). They do not provide a projection onto \(G[2]\). [Stevenhagen 2022, §§2–3](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/128E98F4B96AB15B77CDF63E6E27D447/S0305004121000335a.pdf/redei-reciprocity-governing-fields-and-negative-pell.pdf)

**PROVED:** Once the character’s discriminantal divisor \(d\) is supplied, evaluating it is polynomial time: find a represented value coprime to \(D\), then compute a Kronecker symbol. Thus characters attached to known small discriminantal divisors are public. Supplying the full list of prime-discriminant labels would already disclose the factorization; the meaningful question concerns evaluation with hidden labels or an abstract genus basis.

**PROVED:** There is a precise quadratic-residuosity connection. Set \(D=-4N\). For a prime \(r\) satisfying
\[
r\equiv1\pmod4,\qquad \left(\frac Nr\right)=1,
\]
construct a prime form \(F_r\) of norm \(r\), using a square root of \(D\bmod r\). Then
\[
\chi_{p^*}(F_r)=\left(\frac rp\right),
\qquad
\left(\frac rp\right)\left(\frac rq\right)=1.
\]
Therefore this character is \(+1\) exactly when \(r\) is a quadratic residue modulo \(N\).

**PROVED, conditional on Dirichlet GRH:** This also reduces general quadratic residuosity on
\[
a\in(\mathbf Z/N\mathbf Z)^\times,\qquad (a/N)=1,
\]
to such character evaluations. Find a prime
\[
r\equiv a\pmod N,\qquad r\equiv1\pmod4.
\]
GRH prime-counting estimates permit randomized polynomial-time sampling in this progression using \(r\le N^{2+\varepsilon}\). Construct \(F_r\) and evaluate the hidden character. Its answer is \((a/p)\), which decides residuosity because \((a/p)=(a/q)\).

**PROVED:** Conversely, on represented norms with Jacobi symbol \(+1\), the hidden character bit is exactly quadratic residuosity. When the genus group has an additional public character, extending from its kernel requires choosing a sign on one anchor class. The two choices interchange the hidden \(p\)- and \(q\)-characters. Thus an **unlabelled hidden genus coordinate**, modulo public coordinates and this orientation choice, is closely and explicitly equivalent to residuosity; a fully labelled character is a stronger specification.

**UNVERIFIED:** I do not assert an unconditional polynomial-time equivalence between factoring and genus-character evaluation. In particular, factoring \(\le\) quadratic residuosity is not a known general polynomial-time reduction. Nor does the prime-form argument alone give an unconditional reduction for arbitrary residuosity inputs: constructing the required forms is part of the reduction.

**PROVED:** Even complete genus information does not locate the involutions. In \(C_4\), the nonidentity involution is a square, and every genus character is trivial on it. An exact toy instance is
\[
\operatorname{Cl}(-56)\cong C_4,\qquad
[(3,2,5)]^2=[(2,0,7)].
\]

### 1(b). Square roots: the precise reductions

Define \(\mathrm{SQRT}_D\) to return **some** \(y\) satisfying \(y^2=z\), given a promised square \(z\in G^2\). It must not receive a square-root witness.

**PROVED — abstract square-root reduction:** Suppose \(x\) is uniform in \(G\), put \(z=x^2\), and obtain \(y=\mathrm{SQRT}_D(z)\). Then
\[
u=xy^{-1}\in G[2].
\]
Conditional on \(z\), \(x\) is uniform on its \(T\)-coset. The oracle sees only \(z\), so \(u\) is uniform in \(T\), even if the oracle chooses its answer deterministically. Hence
\[
\Pr[u\notin A]=1-\frac{|A|}{|T|}\ge\frac12.
\]
An oracle succeeding on an \(\epsilon\)-fraction of uniform squares gives success at least
\[
\epsilon\left(1-\frac{|A|}{|T|}\right).
\]

**KNOWN:** For imaginary quadratic forms, a reduced ambiguous representative has one of the boundary conditions
\[
b=0,\qquad |b|=a,\qquad a=c.
\]
For \(D=-4M\), these give explicit factorizations:
\[
M=ac,\qquad
M=u(2c-u)\ \ (a=b=2u),\qquad
M=(a-v)(a+v)\ \ (a=c,\ b=2v).
\]
After stripping known small factors, a non-public ambiguous class gives a factor of \(N\). This is the classical class-group factoring mechanism underlying the square-root reduction. [Hühnlein–Meyer–Takagi 1998, §§3–4](https://www.ecsec.de/fileadmin/Ecsec-files/pub/1998_ICISC.pdf)

**PROVED:** Thus, for the imaginary case,
\[
\mathrm{FACTOR}\le_{\mathrm{rand}}
\mathrm{SQRT}+\text{near-uniform class sampling}.
\]
If sampling is within total variation \(\eta\) of uniform, the success probability changes by at most \(\eta\).

**KNOWN, conditional on Hecke GRH:** Small-prime class-group walks provide polynomial-time near-uniform sampling without knowing \(h\). This makes the preceding reduction polynomial time under GRH. See the expansion theorem and mixing corollary in Jao–Miller–Venkatesan. [JMV 2009, Theorem 1.1 and Corollary 1.3](https://web.williams.edu/Mathematics/sjmiller/public_html/crypto/handouts/JaoMillerVenkatesan_ExpanderGraphsGRHEllCurveCrypto.pdf)

**KNOWN:** In the other direction, Gauss’s ternary-form algorithm computes square roots of imaginary quadratic form classes from the factorization of \(D\), in randomized polynomial time. Randomness supplies quadratic nonresidues modulo the prime factors. Neither \(h\) nor element orders are required:
\[
\mathrm{SQRT}\le_{\mathrm{rand}}\mathrm{FACTOR}.
\]
Hühnlein–Meyer–Takagi describe the algorithm explicitly in §3.1; Lagarias supplies the polynomial complexity analysis. [HMT 1998](https://www.ecsec.de/fileadmin/Ecsec-files/pub/1998_ICISC.pdf)

**UNVERIFIED:** I have not verified an unconditional efficient sampler sufficient to remove the sampling hypothesis from the first reduction for every composite discriminant here. The frequently stated “square roots are equivalent to factoring” should be accompanied by a sampling convention.

**UNVERIFIED:** I also do not transfer the imaginary reduction verbatim to real narrow classes. The abstract extraction of \(G[2]\) remains valid, but finding a useful ambiguous representative within an indefinite cycle introduces an additional algorithmic problem.

### 1(c). Walks, spectral statistics, collisions, and girth

Write \(h=|G|\), \(r=|T|\). Let \(\mu\) be a symmetric lazy generating distribution and
\[
\lambda_\chi=\sum_g\mu(g)\chi(g).
\]

**PROVED — exact formulas:** Fourier inversion gives
\[
\Pr[X_t=e]=\frac1h\sum_{\chi\in\widehat G}\lambda_\chi^t
\]
and
\[
\Pr[X_t\in T]
=\frac rh\sum_{\chi:\,\chi|_T=1}\lambda_\chi^t.
\]
After mixing to total variation \(\eta\),
\[
\Pr[X_t\in T]=\frac rh+O(\eta).
\]
Expansion makes samples inexpensive; it does not increase the stationary density of torsion.

**PROVED:** For independent uniform \(x,y\),
\[
\Pr[x^2=y^2]=\frac rh,\qquad
\Pr[x^2=y^2,\ xy^{-1}\notin A]=\frac{r-|A|}{h}.
\]
Therefore:

| Procedure | Cost in independent near-uniform samples |
|---|---:|
| Directly test \(x^2=e,\ x\notin A\) | \(\Theta(h/(r-|A|))\) |
| Birthday collisions among the \(x^2\) | \(\Theta(\sqrt{h/r})\), with constant useful-collision probability when \(A<T\) |
| Estimate stationary torsion frequency to constant relative accuracy | \(\Theta(h/r)\) |
| Estimate stationary return probability \(1/h\) to constant relative accuracy | \(\Theta(h)\) |

**PROVED:** The birthday assertion follows because squaring maps uniform \(G\) uniformly onto an image of size \(h/r\). Conditional on a square collision, the quotient \(xy^{-1}\) is uniform in \(T\).

**KNOWN:** In the imaginary family with small multipliers, genus theory makes \(r\) at most \(\operatorname{poly}(\log N)\), while the classical class-number estimates give \(h=N^{1/2+o(1)}\). Thus this collision method has \(N^{1/4+o(1)}\) scale.

**PROVED:** The quadratic characters form only \(r\) of the \(h\) spectral terms. Their total contribution to the normalized return formula has magnitude at most \(r/h\). This quantifies one proposed signal, but does not prove that every conceivable spectral statistic needs \(h/r\) samples.

**UNVERIFIED:** I found no spectral statistic that identifies a non-public involution in subexponential time. A universal lower bound for all spectral processing would require an explicit observation model; the stationary estimates above alone do not establish one.

**PROVED — arithmetic girth statement:** Ordinary graph girth is unsuitable: an abelian Cayley graph has commutation cycles of length four. There is, however, a short-relation bound after cancelling these formal relations.

Take distinct unramified split primes \(\ell_i\le B\), choosing one prime ideal above each. If
\[
\prod_i[\mathfrak l_i]^{e_i}=1,\qquad
L=\sum_i|e_i|>0,
\]
replace negative powers by conjugate ideals to obtain an integral principal ideal \((\alpha)\), with
\[
|N(\alpha)|=\prod_i\ell_i^{|e_i|}\le B^L.
\]
The ideal has no rational ideal factor, so \(\alpha\notin\mathbf Z\). Write
\[
\alpha=(u+v\sqrt D)/2,\qquad v\ne0.
\]
For \(D<0\),
\[
|N(\alpha)|=\frac{u^2+|D|v^2}{4}\ge\frac{|D|}{4}.
\]
Consequently,
\[
L\ge\frac{\log(|D|/4)}{\log B}.
\]
This is logarithmic-scale girth after removing formal relations, not a superpolynomial barrier. For real fields the norm inequality fails because of Pell-type cancellation.

### 1(d). Rédei matrices

**KNOWN:** For fundamental \(D\), the Rédei–Reichardt theorem states
\[
r_4=t-1-\operatorname{rank}_{\mathbf F_2}R.
\]
One transposed convention has off-diagonal entries
\[
R_{ij}=\left[\left(\frac{d_j}{\ell_i}\right)\right],
\]
with diagonals making row sums zero. Here \(\ell_i\) is the rational prime underlying \(d_i\), with the appropriate Kronecker convention at \(2\). [Stevenhagen 2022, Theorem 3.1](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/128E98F4B96AB15B77CDF63E6E27D447/S0305004121000335a.pdf/redei-reciprocity-governing-fields-and-negative-pell.pdf)

**PROVED:** Without splitting \(N\), one can compute aggregate symbols such as
\[
(N/\ell)=(p/\ell)(q/\ell),
\]
and entries involving only known primes. These generally do not determine the separate columns indexed by \(p,q\), nor the cross-symbol \((p/q)\). A full matrix with prime-labelled columns already exposes those primes.

**UNVERIFIED:** Computing the scalar \(r_4\) is not thereby proved factoring-hard. Congruences can determine it in restricted families. What fails as a proposed general route is constructing and exploiting the unknown-prime matrix from aggregate Jacobi data.

## 2. Real infrastructure

### 2(a). What giant steps accomplish

**KNOWN:** Infrastructure composition permits fast positioning at a prescribed distance, using compact representations. Baby-step–giant-step methods compute a regulator in roughly
\[
\widetilde O(\sqrt R)
\]
work, giving a \(D^{1/4+o(1)}\) worst-case scale. They do not require smooth ideal norms. [Lenstra, *Solving the Pell equation*, 2008, discussion of infrastructure algorithms](https://library.slmath.org/books/Book44/files/01lenstra.pdf)

**PROVED:** Fast positioning does not solve the square-form search problem. A giant step can skip an interval containing a square form; no available operation tells whether that skipped interval contains one. A square test is a predicate on the encountered coefficients, not an equality collision for which ordinary baby-step–giant-step applies.

**CONJECTURED:** Under the usual random-coefficient model, square norms among reduced ideals of norm about \(\sqrt D\) have density about \(D^{-1/4}\). Sampling distant positions then still needs about \(D^{1/4}\) tests. This density argument is heuristic.

**KNOWN:** Fast return after locating a proper square form is already a recognized SQUFOF improvement. Gower–Wagstaff explicitly distinguish their heuristic average-time analysis from the exact mechanics. Their \(N^{1/4}\) average scale uses stated distribution assumptions, including Assumptions 4.17 and 4.19. [Gower–Wagstaff 2008, Theorem 4.22 and §7](https://homes.cerias.purdue.edu/~ssw/squfof.pdf)

**UNVERIFIED:** No general subexponential giant-step algorithm for locating a proper square form was verified.

### 2(b). Buchmann and Vollmer are relational

**KNOWN:** Buchmann’s method uses a factor base of prime ideals and relations
\[
\prod_i\mathfrak p_i^{e_i}=(\alpha).
\]
Linear dependencies between relation vectors produce units; logarithms of those units yield regulator multiples. Smoothness occurs in generating these relations.

**KNOWN, conditional on GRH:** Vollmer’s 2002 algorithm explicitly constructs relation matrices, solves integer linear systems, obtains units, and computes a real gcd of unit logarithms. Theorem 1 gives regulator approximation with prescribed success probability in
\[
L_D(1/2,3/\sqrt8+\varepsilon),
\]
and a verified class-group/regulator version with constant \(\sqrt2+\varepsilon\). These algorithms fall inside your exclusion. [Vollmer 2002, Theorem 1 and Algorithm 1](https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=0e2fa4e63f97031b938d8b61e2479f2ece24574b&repid=rep1&type=pdf)

### 2(c). Knowing \(R\), a multiple of \(R\), and the midpoint

**KNOWN:** With ordinary class number \(h\), ordinary regulator \(R\), and \(D>0\),
\[
hR=\frac{\sqrt D}{2}L(1,\chi_D).
\]
For the narrow class number and totally positive regulator,
\[
h^+R^+=2hR.
\]
Thus conventions matter by a factor of two. [Lenstra 2008](https://library.slmath.org/books/Book44/files/01lenstra.pdf)

**PROVED:** Let \(C\) denote the circumference of the chosen infrastructure circle. Its midpoint satisfies
\[
C/2=-C/2\pmod C.
\]
Knowing \(C\) allows positioning near this reflection point. It does not ensure that the resulting ambiguity reveals a non-public factorization.

**PROVED — balanced counterexample:** Take
\[
N=731=17\cdot43.
\]
Its continued fraction is
\[
\sqrt{731}=[27;\overline{27,54}],
\]
and its fundamental norm-\(+1\) unit is
\[
\varepsilon=730+27\sqrt{731}.
\]
Nevertheless,
\[
\gcd(730-1,731)=1,\qquad
\gcd(730+1,731)=731.
\]
Moreover,
\[
\sqrt\varepsilon=\frac{27+\sqrt{731}}{\sqrt2},
\qquad
N(27+\sqrt{731})=-2.
\]
The midpoint reflects the public factor \(2\), rather than splitting \(17\cdot43\).

**PROVED — 2-adic issue:** If a certified period multiple is \(T=mC\), then
\[
T/2\equiv
\begin{cases}
0\pmod C,&m\text{ even},\\
C/2\pmod C,&m\text{ odd}.
\end{cases}
\]
After stripping \(2^{v_2(m)}\), halving reaches the midpoint. Halving once is insufficient when \(m\) is even. For \(T=hR\), the relevant multiplier incorporates \(h\) and the chosen orientation convention.

**PROVED:** In an exact circle model, stripping powers of two can be implemented by testing period membership. Numerical approximations need certified precision and compact unit/ideal certificates. A decimal approximation to a large multiple is not, by itself, an exact period-membership oracle.

### 2(d). Exactly what analytic approximation buys

Put \(d=|D|\), and
\[
H=
\begin{cases}
h(D),&D<0,\\
h(D)R(D),&D>0.
\end{cases}
\]
For the orders here, excluding the familiar exceptional unit cases,
\[
H=c_D\sqrt d\,L(1,\chi_D),
\]
with \(c_D=1/\pi\) or \(1/2\).

#### Finishing from an interval

**PROVED:** Suppose
\[
h\in[H_0-\Delta,H_0+\Delta].
\]
For any specified class \(g\), one can find a positive annihilating exponent \(n\) in this interval in
\[
O(\sqrt{\Delta+1}+\log h)
\]
group operations and \(O(\sqrt{\Delta+1})\) storage.

To prove this, cover the integer interval with baby exponents \(j<m\) and giant exponents \(a+im\), taking \(m\asymp\sqrt{\Delta+1}\). A collision
\[
g^{a+im}=g^{-j}
\]
gives \(g^{a+im+j}=1\). Since \(h\) lies in the interval, a collision exists. If baby powers repeat first, their positive exponent difference is already an annihilator.

**PROVED:** This produces an annihilator of \(g\), not necessarily \(h\), its exact order, or a non-public involution. Obtaining sufficient group structure or generators is an additional step.

**PROVED, in the standard infrastructure positioning model:** An interval of width \(O(\Delta)\) containing a period multiple can similarly be searched in
\[
\widetilde O(\sqrt{\Delta+1})
\]
operations to recover a certified nonzero period relation.

For completeness, assume positioning returns a reduced point within \(O(\log D)\) of the requested distance, adjacent gaps are \(O(\log D)\), and the number of baby steps in an interval of length \(s\) is \(O(s+\log D)\). Store the baby interval of length about \(2s\). Position giant points at spacing \(s\) through the target interval. For a period \(T\) in that interval, one giant point has distance minus \(T\) in the stored baby interval, giving an equality and hence a period relation. The cost is
\[
\widetilde O(s+\Delta/s),
\]
minimized at \(s\asymp\sqrt{\Delta+1}\).

#### GRH Euler products

**KNOWN, conditional on Dirichlet GRH:** The prime-character explicit formula and partial summation give, suppressing logarithms,
\[
L(1,\chi_D)
=\prod_{\ell\le X}\left(1-\frac{\chi_D(\ell)}{\ell}\right)^{-1}
+\widetilde O(X^{-1/2}),
\]
for the polynomial-size \(X\) relevant below. Thus
\[
\Delta=\widetilde O(\sqrt d/\sqrt X).
\]
This is the analytic input to the classical Shanks tradeoff; see Schoof, §3. [Schoof, *Quadratic fields and factorization*, 1982](https://www.mat.uniroma2.it/~schoof/mcpaper.pdf)

**PROVED:** Computing the product by direct enumeration costs \(\widetilde O(X)\). The combined cost is
\[
\widetilde O\!\left(X+d^{1/4}X^{-1/4}\right).
\]
Balancing gives
\[
X=d^{1/5},\qquad
\Delta=d^{2/5},\qquad
\text{cost }d^{1/5+o(1)}.
\]
The exponent \(1/5\) is optimal for this displayed tradeoff, not for all possible analytic algorithms.

#### An unconditional randomized approximation

**PROVED:** The same approximation/search tradeoff is available with probabilistic error, without GRH. Here is an elementary derivation.

Let \(\chi\) be a nonprincipal character of known period \(Q\). Its partial sums have absolute value at most \(Q\), so
\[
\left|L(1,\chi)-\sum_{n\le T}\frac{\chi(n)}n\right|
\le\frac{2Q}{T}.
\]
Choose \(T\ge 8Q/\epsilon\). Partition \(1,\ldots,T\) into \(K=O(\log T)\) dyadic blocks. Choose a block uniformly, then \(n\) uniformly within it. If its sampling probability is \(p_n\), the estimator
\[
Z=\frac{\chi(n)}{np_n}
\]
is unbiased for the truncated sum and satisfies \(|Z|\le K\). Averaging
\[
O(K^2\epsilon^{-2}\log(1/\eta))
\]
samples estimates \(L(1,\chi)\) to absolute error \(\epsilon\), with failure probability at most \(\eta\). Each sampled integer has only \(O(\log Q+\log(1/\epsilon))\) bits.

**PROVED:** Therefore absolute precision \(\Delta\) for \(H\) costs
\[
\widetilde O(d/\Delta^2)
\]
character evaluations, and finishing costs \(\widetilde O(\sqrt\Delta)\). Balancing again gives
\[
\Delta=d^{2/5},\qquad
\widetilde O(d^{1/5})
\]
work.

**PROVED:** This unconditional statement concerns a probabilistic approximation followed by a verifiable annihilator/period search. It does not, by itself, certify an exact class number or guarantee a useful factor. It is a relation/order route and remains exponential in \(\log N\).

**UNVERIFIED:** Srinivasan’s 1998 paper reports a related unconditional randomized \(d^{1/5+\varepsilon}\) method. I verified its bibliographic existence but could not inspect the original theorem sufficiently to rely on its precise effectiveness and certification claims.

## 3. Ordinary isogenies and CM

Let \(\mathcal O\) have discriminant \(d<0\), and suppose a suitable ordinary curve with endomorphism ring \(\mathcal O\) is available.

**KNOWN:** The horizontal class-group action is simply transitive on the corresponding CM torsor. For a split auxiliary prime \(s\), the crater cycle length is the order of \([\mathfrak s]\). Vertical movement detects conductor information. [Sutherland, *Isogeny volcanoes*, §3](https://msp.org/obs/2013/1-1/obs-v1-n1-p25-s.pdf)

**PROVED:** Consequently, reaching the opposite point of an even crater by counting around it computes an order/period. Descending a volcano detects the \(s\)-part of the conductor; it does not generally expose the hidden ramified primes \(p,q\). Using the ramified primes themselves as isogeny degrees presupposes discovering them.

**PROVED:** A CM torsor does not supply class inversion as a cheap operation on arbitrary \(j\)-invariants. Negating elliptic-curve points is unrelated to inverting ideal classes. For ordinary CM reduction with principal Frobenius, field Frobenius does not provide the desired hidden inversion operation.

### Constructing the curve

**PROVED:** Your norm equation forces
\[
t=2a,\qquad
\ell=a^2+kNf^2,
\]
so \(\ell\ge kNf^2\). Finding a prime represented by this equation does not construct a curve of the specified trace. Nor is polynomial-time prime search along every such fixed quadratic polynomial established.

**KNOWN:** When the CM points are rational over \(\mathbf F_\ell\), there are \(h(d)\) target \(j\)-invariants, apart from the standard automorphism qualifications. Uniform random \(j\)-sampling therefore hits the desired endomorphism order with probability \(h(d)/\ell\). This follows from the CM torsor description. [Sutherland 2013](https://msp.org/obs/2013/1-1/obs-v1-n1-p25-s.pdf)

**PROVED:** Thus rejection sampling with point counting costs about
\[
\frac{\ell}{h(d)}\operatorname{poly}(\log\ell).
\]
For \(\ell\asymp |d|\) and \(h(d)=|d|^{1/2+o(1)}\), this is \(|d|^{1/2+o(1)}\) trials. Polynomial-time point counting does not remove the rare-trace bottleneck.

**KNOWN:** A degree-\(h\) class polynomial is not logically necessary. Sutherland’s *Accelerating the CM method* constructs a root without necessarily computing its coefficients, using ring-class-field decompositions and CRT. The standard CRT baseline has GRH expected time \(O(|d|\log^{5+\varepsilon}|d|)\); the improvements reduce time by logarithmic factors in suitable cases and can substantially reduce space. They still use class-group structure and enumeration at auxiliary primes. [Sutherland 2012, §§3–4](https://www.cambridge.org/core/services/aop-cambridge-core/content/view/E99A5303B2A403C6D5662BB35A5C8D41/S1461157012001015a.pdf/accelerating-the-cm-method.pdf)

**PROVED:** Writing a full class polynomial modulo \(\ell\) costs at least \(\Omega(h(d)\log\ell)\) output bits. That lower bound does not apply to computing one root.

**UNVERIFIED:** No construction was verified that simultaneously gives the required ordinary curve in subexponential time, avoids the excluded class-group machinery, and makes an ambiguous class accessible without a period or relation computation.

## 4. Non-maximal orders, multipliers, and composita

### Ring class groups

**KNOWN:** For imaginary orders, away from exceptional unit cases,
\[
\pi:\operatorname{Cl}(Dm^2)\twoheadrightarrow\operatorname{Cl}(D)
\]
has kernel size
\[
K=m\prod_{\ell\mid m}\left(1-\frac{(D/\ell)}{\ell}\right).
\]
This is computable from the factorization of \(m\), without splitting \(N\). The kernel comes from a quotient of local unit groups. [Hühnlein 2000, §2, equations (4)–(6)](https://www.ecsec.de/fileadmin/Ecsec-files/pub/2000_Systemsicherheit_IQOSurvey.pdf)

**PROVED:** Newly accessible 2-torsion inside this kernel maps to the identity downstairs. It therefore does not give new 2-torsion in \(\operatorname{Cl}(D)\).

**PROVED:** If \(K\) is odd and \(x\) is already known to map to an involution, then \(x^K\) is an involution with the same image: \(x^2\in\ker\pi\), so \(x^{2K}=1\), and \(\pi(x)^K=\pi(x)\). This lifts a known target; it does not discover one.

**PROVED:** Raising an arbitrary lift to \(K\) does not annihilate the unknown odd part downstairs:
\[
\pi(x^K)=\pi(x)^K.
\]
If a chosen kernel order happens to annihilate that odd part, this is precisely an order-annihilation event.

### The multiplier pencil

**PROVED:** Different squarefree multipliers usually define different quadratic fields. There is no automatic homomorphism between their class groups analogous to the ring-class map. Factoring a small multiplier gives public genus information, but leaves the separate \(p\)- and \(q\)-coordinates unresolved.

**UNVERIFIED:** Searching polynomially many multipliers could exploit correlations in class numbers, regulators, or coefficient distributions. I found no verified subexponential route using such correlations outside the excluded methods. Independent black-box models cannot rule out those arithmetic correlations.

### Biquadratic composita

Let
\[
L=\mathbf Q(\sqrt{-kN},\sqrt{-k'N}).
\]
Its third quadratic subfield is \(\mathbf Q(\sqrt{kk'})\), after removing squares.

**PROVED:** Extension and norm satisfy
\[
N\circ j=[2],\qquad j\circ N=1+\sigma.
\]
For a class extended from the small third field, norming to either large quadratic field gives a principal class: the relevant automorphism acts as conjugation on the third field, and conjugate ideal classes are inverse.

**KNOWN:** Kuroda-type formulas relate the class numbers through a unit index and powers of two. They do not make the unknown large class numbers individually known. [Lemmermeyer, *Kuroda’s class number formula*, 1994](https://citeseerx.ist.psu.edu/document?doi=1c945cba5f04f3031dbe83b3238aa83e57032665&repid=rep1&type=pdf)

**UNVERIFIED:** Explicit compositum units or norm equations might contain useful non-generic information. Computing those objects efficiently is the unresolved step; the formal norm and extension identities alone do not provide a new genus character or involution.

## 5. Generic barriers, with proofs

### Model

The following model is deliberately narrower than explicit quadratic forms.

- Elements have opaque random encodings.
- Operations are multiplication, inversion, equality, and optionally uniform sampling.
- The public subgroup \(A\le G[2]\) and its relations are known.
- Outputs must be handles obtained through this interface.
- Group-dependent coefficient arithmetic, ideal-norm constructors, and analytic information correlated with the hidden order are absent.

A public auxiliary string called \(D\) may be included, but in the distributional theorem it must be independent of the hidden prime.

### Theorem A: torsion output gives a relation

**PROVED:** Suppose the initial non-public handles are \(g_1,\ldots,g_s\). Every computed handle has an efficiently trackable expression
\[
a\prod_{i=1}^s g_i^{v_i},
\qquad a\in A,\quad v_i\in\mathbf Z.
\]
If the output lies in \(G[2]\setminus A\), then
\[
v\ne0,\qquad \prod_i g_i^{2v_i}=1.
\]

**Proof.** Initial handles have basis-vector expressions. Multiplication adds vectors, inversion negates them, and public subgroup factors remain in \(A\). If \(v=0\), the output belongs to \(A\), contrary to success. Squaring the output removes \(a\), since \(a^2=1\), and yields the displayed nonzero relation. After \(T\) operations, coefficients have \(O(T)\) bits. ∎

**PROVED:** A single multivariate relation need not determine any individual order. If \(s\) independent relation vectors form an integer matrix \(V\), then
\[
|\langle g_1,\ldots,g_s\rangle|\mid|\det V|.
\]
Indeed, their lattice lies inside the kernel of \(\mathbf Z^s\to\langle g_i\rangle\), so the kernel index divides the relation-lattice index. This explains both the relevance and the limitation of relation extraction.

### Theorem B: equivalence to an odd order multiple in a cyclic quotient

**PROVED:** Suppose
\[
G=A\times C_{2P},
\]
where \(P\) is odd, and a supplied \(g\) generates the quotient \(G/A\). Without an independent sampling oracle, a successful output yields an odd integer \(m\) divisible by \(P\). Conversely, such an \(m\) produces a successful output.

**Proof.** Track every handle as \(ag^m\). For a successful output,
\[
g^{2m}=1,
\]
so \(P\mid m\). Its quotient is nonidentity, so \(2P\nmid m\); hence \(m\) is odd. Conversely, if \(m\) is odd and \(P\mid m\), then \(g^m\) has nonidentity order-two quotient and square \(1\). ∎

**PROVED:** Uniform sampling does not invalidate this reduction when a public upper bound on \(|G/A|\) is available. Simulate a sample by
\[
a g^u,
\]
with uniform \(a\in A\) and \(u\) uniform in a sufficiently long known interval. Its statistical distance from uniform is at most \(|G/A|/\text{interval length}\). Choose the interval long enough that the total error over all samples is negligible. The exponents remain known, and the simulation has logarithmic exponentiation overhead.

This is a precise order-type equivalence for that family, not for arbitrary class groups.

### Theorem C: an unknown-order lower bound

**PROVED:** Let \(P\) be uniform among primes in \([M,2M]\), and let
\[
G=A\times C_2\times C_P
\]
have random opaque encodings. Give public \(A\) and allow independent uniform samples. Count samples and group operations in a budget \(T\), with at most \(O(T)\) public/input handles. Then
\[
\Pr[\text{output in }G[2]\setminus A]
=O\!\left(\frac{(T+1)^3}{M}\right).
\]
Constant success therefore requires \(\Omega(M^{1/3})\) operations.

**Proof.** Give each sampled handle a formal variable. Until an unexpected equality occurs, every handle is a formal integer vector, together with a known \(A\)-component. After \(T\) operations, vector coefficients have absolute value at most \(2^T\).

Compare two formally distinct handles. Equality requires a nonzero linear equation
\[
\sum_i c_i z_i=0\pmod P
\]
in their independent uniform \(C_P\)-coordinates.

If not all coefficients vanish modulo \(P\), this has probability \(1/P\le1/M\). Otherwise \(P\) divides every coefficient. Choose one nonzero coefficient \(c\). Since \(|c|\le2^{T+1}\), it has at most
\[
\frac{(T+1)\log2}{\log M}
\]
prime divisors in \([M,2M]\). There are \(\Theta(M/\log M)\) primes in that interval. Thus this exceptional case has probability \(O((T+1)/M)\).

There are \(O((T+1)^2)\) pairs of handles. Couple the real computation with its formal simulation; a union bound controls the first unexpected equality by \(O((T+1)^3/M)\).

If no such equality occurs, a successful output has a nonzero formal vector \(v\), by Theorem A. Its square being the identity supplies one further nonzero linear equation, with coefficients \(2v_i\), subject to the same bound. Adding this event proves the result. ∎

### Why not simply \(\Omega(\sqrt P)\)?

**PROVED:** If all compared coefficients, including the final doubled vector, are restricted to absolute value below \(M\), the exceptional divisibility case disappears. The same proof then gives success \(O(T^2/M)\), hence \(\Omega(\sqrt M)\).

**KNOWN:** The usual generic model permits repeated doubling and large exponents, so that restriction is substantial. Sutherland gives an \(O(\sqrt{P/\log\log P})\) generic group-operation bound for prime-order computation, already contradicting a literal universal \(\Omega(\sqrt P)\) claim. His thesis proves an \(\Omega(P^{1/3})\) unknown-order lower bound. [Sutherland 2007, §§2.2 and 4](https://math.mit.edu/~drew/sutherland-phd.pdf)

**KNOWN:** Shoup’s and Maurer’s square-root lower bounds concern discrete logarithms and related tasks; they are not automatic lower bounds for finding an involution. Damgård–Koprowski require hypotheses on the order distribution for root extraction. Rotem–Segev’s generic-ring factoring equivalence concerns RSA-ring repeated squaring, a different interface and problem. [Maurer 2005, Theorem 2](https://crypto.ethz.ch/publications/files/Maurer05.pdf), [Damgård–Koprowski 2002](https://www.iacr.org/archive/eurocrypt2002/23320252/DKea2002.pdf), [Rotem–Segev](https://eprint.iacr.org/2020/812)

### Scope and infrastructure extension

**PROVED:** For several independent groups, the same formal-word argument applies separately, and the collision bound can use the total number of handles. Polynomially many groups do not remove the power lower bound in this artificial distribution.

**UNVERIFIED:** No corresponding distribution theorem was established for the actual class numbers of \(\operatorname{Cl}(\pm4kN)\). In particular, the theorem does not prove that these class groups have the required large random odd prime factor.

**PROVED:** In an exact infrastructure-circle model with a tracked distance lift \(x\), finding a nonzero reflection-fixed point gives
\[
2x=mC
\]
for some nonzero integer \(m\): a period relation. This covers midpoint-finding in that model.

**PROVED:** It does not cover SQUFOF’s square-norm event. Constructing an ideal square root from explicit coefficients introduces a class through an arithmetic constructor absent from the model. Treating that constructor as ordinary black-box composition would assume away the proposed escape.

## 6. Non-generic features and their present status

| Feature | Status and consequence |
|---|---|
| Canonical reduced coefficients | **KNOWN:** Permit direct ambiguity tests and gcd extraction. No verified general subexponential search for the useful coefficients. |
| Forms of specified norm | **KNOWN:** Constructible efficiently for small split prime norms. Prescribing an arbitrary norm may require modular square roots or its factorization. |
| Small primes | **KNOWN, GRH:** Give generators and rapid mixing. Mixing supplies random classes, not a torsion projection. |
| Integer norms and smooth norms | **KNOWN:** Smooth norms yield factor-base relations and conditional subexponential algorithms; excluded here. |
| Square integer norms | **KNOWN:** Enable explicit ideal halving in SQUFOF. This escapes the generic interface. The fast search remains **UNVERIFIED**. |
| Infrastructure distances | **KNOWN:** Enable compact positioning, regulator search, and fast return. A useful midpoint is not guaranteed. |
| Public analytic \(L\)-values | **PROVED above:** Give the \(d^{1/5}\) approximation/search tradeoff. They violate the independent-public-string premise of the generic lower bound. |
| Factorization-dependent Gauss descent | **KNOWN:** Computes square roots without learning \(h\). For prime discriminants this is a genuine non-generic escape from generic root hardness; it supplies no hidden involution when \(h\) is odd. |
| Ring-class maps and local kernels | **KNOWN:** Give controlled new kernel structure. Kernel torsion does not create new quotient torsion. |
| Compositum units and norm equations | **UNVERIFIED:** Potential arithmetic information, but no verified fast construction yielding the requested target. |
| CM \(j\)-invariants and volcanoes | **KNOWN:** Realize actions and conductor tests. No verified cheap involution operation or suitable subexponential setup meeting the exclusions. |

